  "content": "• Meaning of cell division and structure of chromosomes in organisms\n• Types of cell division (mitosis and meiosis)\n• Significance of cell division in organisms (include tissue culture)\n• Budding and binary fission",
  "learningOutcomes": "By the end of the sub-strand, the learner should be able to:\na) explain the significance of mitosis and meiosis in organisms\nb) illustrate the stages of mitosis and meiosis in cells\nc) distinguish between budding and binary fission in relation to cell division\nd) recognise the importance of cell division in reproduction, growth, and development.",
  "coreCompetencies": "• Creativity and Imagination: The learner innovatively models budding in yeast and binary fission in bacteria.\n• Digital Literacy: The learner enhances computing skills while watching animations showing various stages of mitosis and meiosis.\n• Communication and Collaboration: Learners work in groups to observe cell diagrams, build clay models and present findings to peers.\n• Critical Thinking and Problem Solving: Learners analyse chromosome numbers and case studies to decide whether mitosis or meiosis is involved.\n• Learning to Learn: Learners revise their initial ideas and models as new evidence emerges, and track their progress on the Driving Question Board.\n• Self-efficacy: Learners build confidence by explaining their own growth using evidence they gathered themselves.",
  "values": "• Unity: The learner collaborates with others while using a light microscope to observe stages of meiosis and mitosis in living tissues.\n• Respect: The learner appreciates others' efforts while presenting illustrations to show the behaviour of chromosomes in various stages of cell division.\n• Integrity: Learners record observations honestly, even when these differ from expected results or from the textbook.",
  "sep": "• Asking Questions and Defining Problems: Learners generate questions about their own growth and post them on the Driving Question Board.\n• Developing and Using Models: Learners build clay models of 1 cell → 2 cells, draw chromosome behaviour and revise a cumulative model each lesson.\n• Planning and Carrying Out Investigations: Learners prepare and observe onion root tip slides under a light microscope.\n• Analyzing and Interpreting Data: Learners analyse cell counts, observation tables and chromosome numbers before and after division.\n• Using Mathematics and Computational Thinking: Learners calculate chromosome numbers after fertilisation (46 + 46 = 92 versus 23 + 23 = 46).\n• Constructing Explanations: Learners explain how mitosis and meiosis account for growth, repair and reproduction.\n• Engaging in Argument from Evidence: Learners justify with evidence which type of cell division operates in each case study.\n• Obtaining, Evaluating and Communicating Information: Learners research chromosome structure and stage names from texts and animations, then present to peers.",
