  "careers": "• Medical Doctor / Paediatrician: monitors child growth and development, which depends on cell division.\n• Oncologist / Cancer Researcher: studies uncontrolled mitosis in cancer, e.g. at Kenyatta National Hospital.\n• Geneticist / Genetic Counsellor: applies knowledge of meiosis to inheritance and chromosome disorders.\n• Plant Tissue Culture Technician: multiplies disease-free bananas, tea and potatoes (e.g. KALRO, Kericho tea estates).\n• Laboratory Technologist / Microbiologist: studies bacterial binary fission and yeast budding.\n• Agricultural Officer / Livestock Breeder: uses reproduction and cell division knowledge to improve crops and herds.",
  "focus": "This unit uses the puzzle of a child growing from infancy to adolescence to drive learners to discover that cells divide to produce new cells. Learners gather evidence from diagrams, models, readings, animations and microscope work to build understanding of chromosomes, mitosis, meiosis and asexual reproduction (budding and binary fission). Names of stages and terms are introduced only after learners have described what they observe, and learners revise a class model across the unit.",
  "phenomenon": "A child grows from infancy to adolescence. Learners compare baby photographs of themselves with how they look now and watch a time-lapse of a child growing up over 14 years. A newborn baby weighing about 3 kg becomes a teenager of 50 kg or more, so the body must produce an enormous number of new cells. It is puzzling because we only eat food, yet our bodies make billions of new cells. Where do all these cells come from, and how does each new cell get the right instructions to work properly?",
  "supportingPhenomena": "• A cut on the skin of a child playing football in Kisumu heals within days, with new skin cells replacing the damaged ones.\n• A maize seedling or sukuma wiki plant produces new roots and shoots, and a farmer in the Rift Valley sees crops grow taller each week.\n• Brothers and sisters from the same parents look similar but are not identical, and sperm and egg cells must each carry 23 chromosomes.\n• Yeast used to make mandazi dough rises and bubbles as it multiplies, and a single bacterium can become millions in hours, so one organism can make new organisms without sex cells.",
  "drivingQuestion": "DRIVING QUESTION: How does the body produce the enormous number of new cells needed for growth?\n\nKICD KEY INQUIRY QUESTIONS:\n1. How is cell division important?",
  "storylineThread": "Lesson 1: Students are introduced to the phenomenon. They watch a time-lapse of growing up over 14 years, make initial drawings and write their initial ideas on fullscaps, then generate questions and post them on the Driving Question Board (How does growth occur?).\nLesson 2: Meaning of cell division and structure of the chromosome. Groups observe and analyse diagrams of cells before and after division and record counts in a table. They build a 1 cell → 2 cells clay model and propose what must be passed on, then label an unlabelled chromosome diagram using a reading. Question raised: What happens to the chromosomes during cell division to ensure that each new cell gets the correct genetic information?\nLesson 3: Types of cell division: mitosis and its stages. Groups describe chromosome movement in a mitosis diagram first, then match their observations to prophase, metaphase, anaphase, telophase and cytokinesis using a reading. They return to the phenomenon to explain how mitosis helps a child grow. Question raised: If mitosis produces new body cells similar to the original, how are the cells used for sexual reproduction produced?\nLesson 4: Meiosis and its stages. Groups solve the chromosome-number problem (46 + 46), sequence unlabelled pictures of meiosis I and II, use a reading and videos to label the stages, then explain why sperm and egg cells have 23 chromosomes. Question raised: What would happen to an organism if mitosis and meiosis did not occur?\nLesson 5: Significance of mitosis and meiosis. Groups investigate case studies (child growing, skin healing, plant roots and shoots, chicken producing offspring, parents producing children with different characteristics) and complete a table linking each to mitosis or meiosis with evidence. Question raised: How can a single organism produce a new organism without producing sex cells?\nLesson 6: Budding and binary fission. Learners compare the two processes, model budding in yeast and binary fission in bacteria, present the main differences, and conclude that both are asexual reproduction from a single parent without fusion of sex cells.\nLesson 7: Microscope practical on mitosis. Learners prepare and observe onion root tip slides with a light microscope following safety precautions, identify stages of mitosis, and link their observations to growth in the phenomenon.\nLesson 8: Significance of cell division in organisms, including tissue culture. Learners explore how cell division supports growth, repair, reproduction and development, and how tissue culture multiplies plants such as bananas and tea in Kenya.\nLesson 9: Consolidation and digital animations. Learners watch animations of mitosis and meiosis, compare mitosis and meiosis in a table, and present their chromosome-behaviour models and illustrations to peers, giving respectful feedback.\nLesson 10: Final model and return to the phenomenon. Learners complete and present their final cumulative model, revisit the Driving Question Board and their initial drawings, and write an evidence-based explanation of how the body produces the enormous number of new cells needed for a child to grow."
